[{:seon.task/name "one-monotonic-clock"
  :seon.task/plan "Done; what exists. Seon has one monotonic clock: `seon.profile/now-ns`, uncontracted (seon.profile is not metered), a Chez foreign read of clock_gettime_nsec_np(CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW), the clock every process shares (a forked child reads the same timeline): 9.5 ns a read, no allocation, 41 ns resolution on the M1, against Jolt's System/nanoTime at 102 ns and 225 bytes through its contract. `seon.profile/ms-since` is the one 'ms since t0' (it lives in seon.profile because seon.contract and seon.db cannot require seon.lib). `seon.lib/now-ns`, `seon.sock/now-ns` and every System/nanoTime in src and test are gone; the profiler's samples are no longer quantized to 1 us. v1: System/nanoTime on the JVM, one clock within one process. Wiki: docs/machine.md#the-clock."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.profile-test/the-clock-is-the-one-every-process-reads seon.profile-test/reading-the-clock-allocates-nothing seon.profile-test/every-touched-function-has-an-estimated-time}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-counts-are-its-own"
  :seon.task/plan "What was seen, once, 2026-11-05: seon.profile-test/a-functions-own-work-is-its-work-less-its-callees read (= 442 362) and (= 0 -496) in bin/check's agent-tests step on jolt/render-speaks at 7a2c32cba6, green at the commit before it with only docs changed, and it has not been reproduced. A leaf's self bytes below zero can only come from a counted call running inside it: seon.profile's two kid counters are process-wide, and its docstring says self is exact on one thread that does not park. Not yet known: what ran. To look at first: agent-tests runs tests in an agent's test fork, where seon.proofs/run-tests! runs a test under Jolt's run-interruptible and tells root as it goes (a fiber that writes may run when the test yields), and a signal handler not under seon.profile/aside; a thread or fiber left by an earlier test. Why it matters past this test: seon.profile/calls is process-wide too, so a cost proof in that process could count another's calls, and a verdict is reused by digest. What should be true: a test's counts are its own, whatever else its process runs, or the counts say they were interleaved. A fix that only re-runs or loosens this test leaves the class."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.profile-test/a-functions-own-work-is-its-work-less-its-callees}}]
